  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThrough Wednesday...\n\nMid and upper ridging centered over the southwestern CONUS and \nRockies, paired with an increasingly moist low level airmass, will\nresult in hot and humid conditions over the next few days, with \nhigh temps approaching the triple digits in some areas and heat \nindices over 105F and exceeding 110F at times. During the \novernight, temperatures will be above average with lows in the \nupper 70s in most areas, and possibly around 80 in some spots \nTuesday night/Wed morning. A Heat Advisory remains in effect \nareawide, except in the upper Delta where an Extreme Heat Warning \nis in effect. These were both expanded the Extreme Heat through\ncentral MS and NW LA through Tuesday evening.\n\nAs we are on the eastern periphery of the upper ridge, there will\nbe isolated to scattered mainly diurnally driven convection each \nday, with chances hovering around 20% through mid week which will \nprovide at least temporary heat relief for some sites that see\nprecipitation. \n\nA few storms with strong wind gusts cannot be ruled out, but \nthere are no signs of organized severe potential worthy of an \noutlook at this time. \n\nTD 2 is located over the northeastern Gulf today. In spite of its\nrelatively close proximity, the odds of hazardous impacts to our \narea continue to remain very low. The upper ridge resulting in the\nheat impacts locally will expand through the week, steering the \nsystem westward. Additionally, it appears increasing shear may \ndisplace much of the convection to the south side of the system. \nBased on the current forecast track, showers could make it up to \nthe Hwy 98 corridor during the middle of this week, with \nperiodically breezy conditions and gusts in the 20-30 mph range. \nAgain however, the threat for impactful conditions this far north \nremains quite low.\n\nThursday through the weekend...\n\nA digging Atlantic trough will bring a weak surface front \nsouthward into the area, where it will stall and eventually wash \nout. This feature will help to cut a few degrees off high temps \nthrough the back half of the week and bring dewpoints down a \ncouple of degrees, providing at least a little bit of relief; but \ndon't expect temperatures to be unseasonable for July. With the \nfront not expected to push all the way through, daytime heat \nstress levels will still remain elevated for areas south of I-20 \nthrough the end of the week. /DL/OAJ/",
